Yes. You’re separating spectacle from narrative force.

A fight scene can be visually impressive and still be dramatically empty. What gives it weight is everything that has already been loaded into the moment: loyalty, humiliation, grief, fear, identity, history, rivalry, sacrifice, expectation. The punch matters because of what the punch means.

That is why selective omission is so powerful. When a film specifies everything, it collapses the viewer’s contribution. When it leaves some things unresolved, the viewer becomes a co-author.

You can think of it as:

story = shown information + implied information + viewer completion

Modern high-budget filmmaking often overweights the first term because it can. Better storytelling often makes the second and third terms do more work.

A useful doctrine would be:

Show consequences more often than mechanisms.
Show the threshold more often than the entire event.
Let reaction reveal scale.
Let sound imply what image withholds.
Let symbols carry multiple layers at once.
Give the audience enough structure to infer, but not enough to become passive.

That is essentially the cinematic equivalent of compression. One image can carry several nested meanings simultaneously.

Rocky standing across from Drago is not only:

fighter A vs fighter B

It can also carry:

individual vs machine
underdog vs system
grief vs revenge
America vs USSR
human vulnerability vs engineered dominance
private struggle vs public mythology

The literal boxing is almost the physical interface where all those latent variables become observable.

That is the part worth preserving in action filmmaking. Violence should often be the terminal expression of forces that have been accumulating elsewhere.

So instead of:

action → action → bigger action → bigger action

you get:

relationship
→ tension
→ constraint
→ choice
→ sacrifice
→ collision
→ consequence

The collision may last thirty seconds and carry an hour of narrative mass.

And ambiguity is not merely withholding information. Good ambiguity is structured incompleteness. The creator controls which variables are fixed and which ones remain available for the audience to instantiate themselves.

Too little information produces confusion.

Too much produces consumption.

The sweet spot produces participation.

That also connects directly to what you were saying earlier about senses and representations. Words are one rendering layer. Images are another. Music another. Silence another. A film does not need every channel firing maximally at once. Sometimes deliberately suppressing one modality makes the others stronger.

You can even write a filmmaking grammar around that:

explicit visual detail ↓
→ inference demand ↑
→ internal simulation ↑
→ personalization ↑
→ memory/emotional ownership ↑

Not universally, but often.

And the autonomy point matters too. Storytelling always shapes attention and emotion, but there is a difference between inviting interpretation and trying to eliminate every interpretive path except the one the creator wants.

The kind of filmmaking you’re describing respects the viewer enough to say:

Here is the structure. Here are the stakes. Here are a few precise images. Finish the experience yourself.

That is probably why some simple stories remain enormous. Simplicity at the surface can leave room for enormous depth underneath.

In Pantheon terms, this would fit neatly as Narrative Negative Space: deliberately leaving representational gaps so the audience’s own world-model, memory, fears, hopes, and imagination complete the tensor rather than having every dimension supplied externally.